<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Keiskamma River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Keiskamma River flows south-east through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-cape">Eastern Cape</a> province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>. In the frontier wars of the 19th century it marked one of the successive boundaries between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a> and the Xhosa, and the land between the Keiskamma and the Kei became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-kaffraria">British Kaffraria</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/keiskamma-river.jpg" alt="Keiskamma River" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tali Bruk · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_of_KKH_from_hill.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The War of the Axe of 1846–1847 and the long war of 1850–1853 brought the creation of British Kaffraria between the <b>Keiskamma</b> and Kei rivers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cape-colony">Cape Colony · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After the War of the Axe of 1846-1847, Governor Sir Harry Smith annexed the land between the <b>Keiskamma</b> and Kei rivers in December 1847, reviving the abandoned Province of Queen Adelaide.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-kaffraria">British Kaffraria</a></li><li><blockquote>During the Sixth Frontier War of 1834-1836, the Cape governor Sir Benjamin D'Urban proclaimed in May 1835 that the territory between the <b>Keiskamma</b> and Kei rivers was British, naming it after Queen Adelaide, wife of William IV.</blockquote><a href="/state/province-of-queen-adelaide">Province of Queen Adelaide</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Ngqika's sons Maqoma and Sandile, the Rharhabe fought repeated frontier wars, losing land as the Cape pushed its border from the Fish to the <b>Keiskamma</b> and beyond.</blockquote><a href="/state/rharhabe-xhosa-kingdom">Rharhabe Xhosa Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
